-- #1641 — A CONSTRAINT WRAPPER ON AN IMPL HEAD SUPPRESSES THE AMBIGUITY REJECTION THE
-- IDENTICAL PLAIN HEAD RECEIVES. `zero`'s receiver here is undetermined (`ignore2 : a ->
-- Int` discards its argument), and there are TWO impls of `Zero`, so this program is
-- exactly the shape the ambiguity rule exists to reject — and with a plain `impl Zero Int`
-- head it IS rejected, at `check`, exit 1 (plain-head-rejected.mdk, not graded). Wrapping
-- the head in `Eq x =>` makes `check` accept it silently at exit 0; `medaka build` then
-- dies inside the emitter.
--
-- ⭐ HAND-DERIVED, NOT CAPTURED — and the derivation is about WHAT MUST NOT HAPPEN, which
-- is what makes it checkable without trusting the engine:
--
--   1. `ignore2 : a -> Int` is fully polymorphic in its argument and returns 5 for any
--      argument, so the occurrence `zero` in `ignore2 zero` imposes NO constraint on which
--      `Zero` impl is meant. Its receiver type is undetermined, full stop.
--   2. `Zero` has TWO impls whose heads are at DIFFERENT types (`Int` and `Bool`) with
--      DIFFERENT method bodies (`0` and `False`). Two candidates, nothing to choose
--      between them.
--   3. ⇒ THERE IS NO CORRECT VALUE FOR THIS PROGRAM TO COMPUTE. A compiler must refuse it,
--      exactly as it refuses the plain-head sibling. Both impls must count, and the only
--      difference between the sibling and this file is a wrapper on one head — a wrapper
--      cannot make an impl stop existing.
--   4. ⇒ ACCEPTING AT `check` IS THE DEFECT. That `medaka build` then panics is the loud
--      consequence of the same acceptance, and it is the channel this fixture pins because
--      it is the one that carries an exact, distinctive observable.
--
-- ⚠️ TWO IMPLS ARE LOAD-BEARING. `checkUndeterminedObligation`'s rule is guarded on
-- `implCountForIfaceU >= 2`, so a ONE-IMPL program cannot reach the arm at all — that is
-- exactly why an earlier attempt to observe this residual (PR #1638) failed and was
-- recorded as unobservable. Do not "simplify" this file by deleting `impl Zero Bool`.
--
-- ⚠️ IT IS NOT SPECIFIC TO THE CONSTRAINT WRAPPER: `impl Zero (<Stdout> Int)` behaves
-- identically (measured — see effect-wrapper.mdk, not graded). The defect is a property of
-- the head projection the impl census reads, not of `TyConstrained`.
interface Zero a where
  zero : a

impl Zero (Eq x => Int) where
  zero = 0

impl Zero Bool where
  zero = False

ignore2 : a -> Int
ignore2 _ = 5

main = println (ignore2 zero)
